-- title:
--   Analytic chart from an étale coordinate on a smooth curve
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain and a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra of finite type, assume $S$ is smooth over $\mathbb{C}$ in the sense of `Algebra.Smooth`, and assume the $S$-module $\Omega_{S/\mathbb{C}} =$ `KaehlerDifferential ℂ S` has rank $1$. Let $\sigma_0 : S \to \mathbb{C}$ be a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra homomorphism and $t \in S$ an element whose differential $d t$ does not lie in $\mathfrak{m}_{\sigma_0} \cdot \Omega_{S/\mathbb{C}}$, where $\mathfrak{m}_{\sigma_0} = \ker \sigma_0$ and the product is the submodule generated by $\mathfrak{m}_{\sigma_0}$ times all of $\Omega_{S/\mathbb{C}}$. The conclusion produces a radius $r > 0$ and a set $\mathcal{U}$ of $\mathbb{C}$-algebra homomorphisms $S \to \mathbb{C}$ such that: $\sigma_0 \in \mathcal{U}$; the evaluation map $\sigma \mapsto \sigma(t)$ is a bijection from $\mathcal{U}$ onto the open disc $B(\sigma_0(t), r) \subseteq \mathbb{C}$; for every $s \in S$ there is a function $F : \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{C}$, complex differentiable on $B(\sigma_0(t), r)$, with $\sigma(s) = F(\sigma(t))$ for all $\sigma \in \mathcal{U}$; and $\mathcal{U}$ is open for the topology of pointwise convergence of values, in the explicit form that each $\sigma \in \mathcal{U}$ admits a finite set $fs \subseteq S$ and an $\varepsilon > 0$ with every $\sigma'$ satisfying $\|\sigma'(s) - \sigma(s)\| < \varepsilon$ for all $s \in fs$ again lying in $\mathcal{U}$.
--
--   This is the holomorphic implicit function theorem in algebraic dress: near a $\mathbb{C}$-point of a smooth affine curve over $\mathbb{C}$, an element $t$ whose differential generates $\Omega_{S/\mathbb{C}}$ at that point serves as an analytic coordinate, and every element of $S$ becomes a holomorphic function of it. It underlies the analytic structure on the complex points of such a curve, and is used for the local period chart on Čerednik–Drinfeld quaternionic moduli and for connectedness and convergence arguments on $X(\mathbb{C})$.
